1
00:00:02,043 --> 00:00:03,671
Oh, Ross, Mon,
is it okay if I bring someone

2
00:00:03,712 --> 00:00:05,005
to your parents'
anniversary party?

3
00:00:05,046 --> 00:00:06,256
Sure.
Sure, yeah.

4
00:00:06,298 --> 00:00:07,341
Who's the guy?

5
00:00:07,382 --> 00:00:08,634
Well, his name's Parker

6
00:00:08,676 --> 00:00:10,177
and I met him
at the dry cleaners.

7
00:00:10,218 --> 00:00:13,138
Ooh, did he put a little starch
in your bloomers?

8
00:00:15,516 --> 00:00:16,809
Who said that?

9
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:19,728
No, he's really great, though.

10
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:22,940
He has this
incredible zest for life.

11
00:00:22,982 --> 00:00:24,608
And he treats me like a queen.

12
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:25,943
Except at night

13
00:00:25,985 --> 00:00:28,111
when he treats me like
the naughty girl I am.

14
00:00:29,571 --> 00:00:30,865
Oh, by the way.

15
00:00:30,906 --> 00:00:32,115
Would it be okay
if I give the toast

16
00:00:32,157 --> 00:00:33,325
to Mom and Dad
this year?

17
00:00:33,367 --> 00:00:34,576
Uh, yeah.
You sure you want to

18
00:00:34,618 --> 00:00:36,286
after what happened
at their 20th?

19
00:00:36,328 --> 00:00:37,705
Yeah, I'd
really like to.

20
00:00:37,746 --> 00:00:39,080
Okay.

21
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:40,582
Hopefully this time
Mom won't boo you.

22
00:00:40,624 --> 00:00:41,625
Yes!

23
00:00:41,667 --> 00:00:43,502
Every year Ross makes a toast

24
00:00:43,544 --> 00:00:46,630
and it's always really moving
and always makes them cry.

25
00:00:46,672 --> 00:00:48,590
Well this year,
I'm going to make them cry.

26
00:00:48,632 --> 00:00:51,510
And you, you wonder why
Ross is their favorite?

27
00:00:52,511 --> 00:00:54,638
No, really,
anytime Ross makes a toast

28
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:57,892
everyone cries and hugs him
and pats him on the back

29
00:00:57,933 --> 00:00:59,476
and they all
come up to me and say

30
00:00:59,518 --> 00:01:02,646
"God, your brother."

31
00:01:02,688 --> 00:01:04,439
You know what they're
going to say this year?

32
00:01:04,481 --> 00:01:07,359
"God, you."

33
00:01:08,694 --> 00:01:12,239
Well, I can promise you at least
one person will be crying.

34
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:13,657
You know, I'm an actor

35
00:01:13,699 --> 00:01:16,284
and any actor worth his salt
can cry on cue.

36
00:01:16,326 --> 00:01:17,661
Really, you
can do that?

37
00:01:17,703 --> 00:01:19,371
Oh, are you kidding me?
Watch.

38
00:01:30,382 --> 00:01:32,593
Well, I can't do it
with you guys watching me.

39
00:01:40,475 --> 00:01:44,688
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

40
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:46,941
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

41
00:01:46,982 --> 00:01:49,068
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

42
00:01:49,109 --> 00:01:53,906
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

43
00:01:53,948 --> 00:01:56,199
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

44
00:01:56,241 --> 00:01:59,494
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

45
00:01:59,536 --> 00:02:02,748
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

46
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:06,251
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

47
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:15,385
What are you doing?

48
00:02:15,427 --> 00:02:17,721
Oh, I'm working on
my toast for the party.

49
00:02:17,763 --> 00:02:19,848
Or as I like to call it

50
00:02:19,890 --> 00:02:21,475
"Sob-Fest 2002."

51
00:02:22,517 --> 00:02:23,769
Hey, check this out.

52
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:24,853
It's a dog.

53
00:02:24,895 --> 00:02:26,271
It's a dead dog.

54
00:02:27,689 --> 00:02:28,816
That's Chi-Chi.

55
00:02:28,857 --> 00:02:30,818
She died when I
was in high school.

56
00:02:30,859 --> 00:02:32,152
It's your parents
anniversary and you're

57
00:02:32,193 --> 00:02:33,361
going to talk about
their dead pet?

58
00:02:33,403 --> 00:02:34,905
It's good stuff, huh?

59
00:02:37,032 --> 00:02:38,033
Hi.

60
00:02:38,075 --> 00:02:39,534
Hey.

61
00:02:39,576 --> 00:02:40,953
Aw, Joey, you got
a present for my parents?

62
00:02:40,995 --> 00:02:41,954
That's so sweet.

63
00:02:41,996 --> 00:02:43,246
Yeah, yeah, look.

64
00:02:43,288 --> 00:02:44,915
In honor of their
35th wedding anniversary

65
00:02:44,957 --> 00:02:46,416
I had a star
named after them.

66
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:48,043
Oh, that is so cool.

67
00:02:48,085 --> 00:02:49,461
And

68
00:02:49,503 --> 00:02:52,006
I got them a book on
Kama Sutra for the elderly.

69
00:02:53,715 --> 00:02:55,717
Hey, do you guys
have any extra ribbon?

70
00:02:55,759 --> 00:02:57,845
Oh, yeah, sure.
What do you need?

71
00:02:57,886 --> 00:03:01,015
We've got, uh, lace,
satin, satiné, raffia

72
00:03:01,056 --> 00:03:02,474
gingham, felt...

73
00:03:04,059 --> 00:03:06,603
And I think my testicles
may be in here, too.

74
00:03:09,397 --> 00:03:11,984
Oh, Chi-Chi.

75
00:03:12,026 --> 00:03:15,779
Oh, I love this dog.

76
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:17,781
You know, uh, Monica
couldn't get braces

77
00:03:17,823 --> 00:03:19,574
because Chi-Chi needed
knee surgery.

78
00:03:19,616 --> 00:03:20,575
Oh.

79
00:03:20,617 --> 00:03:22,577
What?

80
00:03:22,619 --> 00:03:25,455
You were the 200 pound
11-year-old who rode her.

81
00:03:30,335 --> 00:03:31,419
Hey.

82
00:03:31,461 --> 00:03:33,088
Everybody,
this is Parker.

83
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:34,255
Parker, this...

84
00:03:34,297 --> 00:03:35,632
No, no, wait,
don't tell me.

85
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:36,633
Let me guess.

86
00:03:36,675 --> 00:03:38,052
Joey, Monica

87
00:03:38,093 --> 00:03:39,386
Ross, Rachel,
and...

88
00:03:39,427 --> 00:03:41,471
I'm sorry,
Phoebe didn't mention you.

89
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:45,433
Chandler! I'm kidding.
Already you're my favorite.

90
00:03:45,475 --> 00:03:47,644
( laughs )

91
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:48,854
Why don't each of you

92
00:03:48,896 --> 00:03:49,980
tell me a little bit
about yourselves?

93
00:03:50,022 --> 00:03:51,106
Uh, actually, I'm sorry.

94
00:03:51,148 --> 00:03:52,399
We probably should
get going.

95
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:54,026
( laughs )

96
00:03:54,068 --> 00:03:55,819
Classic Ross.

97
00:03:58,155 --> 00:03:59,114
Rachel

98
00:03:59,156 --> 00:04:00,866
Rachel, look
how you glow.

99
00:04:00,908 --> 00:04:01,950
May I?

100
00:04:01,992 --> 00:04:03,410
Well... I think
you already are.

101
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:04,703
( chuckles )

102
00:04:04,745 --> 00:04:08,082
Rachel, you have life
growing inside you.

103
00:04:08,123 --> 00:04:11,001
Is there anything in this
world more miraculous

104
00:04:11,043 --> 00:04:12,794
than... ooh,
a picture of a dog.

105
00:04:12,836 --> 00:04:13,962
Whose is this?

106
00:04:15,505 --> 00:04:16,548
That's my old dog.

107
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:18,008
He passed away
years ago.

108
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:21,136
Oh, well, at least you
were lucky to have him.

109
00:04:21,178 --> 00:04:23,304
Bow-wow, old friend.

110
00:04:23,346 --> 00:04:25,099
Bow-wow.

111
00:04:27,059 --> 00:04:28,102
So, where's the party?

112
00:04:28,143 --> 00:04:29,144
It's out on
the island.

113
00:04:29,186 --> 00:04:30,311
It's in
Massapequa.

114
00:04:30,353 --> 00:04:31,479
Mmmm-assapequa.

115
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:33,356
It sounds like
a magical place.

116
00:04:33,398 --> 00:04:34,816
Tell me about Massapequa.

117
00:04:34,858 --> 00:04:36,526
Is it steeped
in Native American history?

118
00:04:36,568 --> 00:04:40,781
Well, there is an Arby's
in the shape of a tepee.

119
00:04:40,822 --> 00:04:42,365
Okay, I've got my note cards.

120
00:04:42,407 --> 00:04:43,575
Um, you got the presents?
Yeah.

121
00:04:43,617 --> 00:04:44,701
And I've got the car keys.

122
00:04:44,743 --> 00:04:45,702
We're driving?
Yeah.

123
00:04:45,744 --> 00:04:46,453
Aces!

124
00:04:46,494 --> 00:04:47,495
Ooh.

125
00:04:50,331 --> 00:04:52,167
So, uh, he seems
like a nice guy.

126
00:04:52,209 --> 00:04:53,168
Yeah, yeah.

127
00:04:53,210 --> 00:04:54,502
I like him a lot.

128
00:04:54,544 --> 00:04:56,337
You want to hang back
and take our own cab?

129
00:04:56,379 --> 00:04:57,881
Yeah, otherwise,
I'm not going.

130
00:05:04,512 --> 00:05:05,555
Oh!

131
00:05:05,597 --> 00:05:06,598
Hey!

132
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:07,766
You look
so great.

133
00:05:07,808 --> 00:05:09,517
Hey, Ma.

134
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:11,436
This is such
a great party.

135
00:05:11,478 --> 00:05:12,520
35 years.

136
00:05:12,562 --> 00:05:14,481
Very impressive.

137
00:05:14,522 --> 00:05:16,775
Do you guys have
any pearls of wisdom?

138
00:05:16,817 --> 00:05:18,152
Jack?

139
00:05:18,193 --> 00:05:20,695
Why would you serve food
on such a sharp stick?

140
00:05:21,947 --> 00:05:24,241
It's a good question, Dad.

141
00:05:24,283 --> 00:05:25,826
It's a good question.

142
00:05:27,368 --> 00:05:29,537
Congratulations,
you two.

143
00:05:29,579 --> 00:05:30,831
BOTH:
Oh, thank you.

144
00:05:30,872 --> 00:05:32,291
We're
so excited.

145
00:05:32,332 --> 00:05:34,542
And also, congratulations
on your wedding.

146
00:05:34,584 --> 00:05:36,544
What?

147
00:05:36,586 --> 00:05:38,505
Can we talk to you
for just a minute?

148
00:05:38,546 --> 00:05:39,589
Yeah.

149
00:05:41,591 --> 00:05:43,927
It's just
a little thing.

150
00:05:43,969 --> 00:05:46,013
While we think it's
simply marvelous

151
00:05:46,054 --> 00:05:48,807
that you're having
this baby out of wedlock...

152
00:05:50,433 --> 00:05:53,561
some of our friends
are less open-minded.

153
00:05:53,603 --> 00:05:55,522
Which is why we've told
them all you're married.

154
00:05:55,563 --> 00:05:56,606
BOTH:
What?!

155
00:05:56,648 --> 00:05:58,441
Thanks for going
along with this.

156
00:05:58,483 --> 00:05:59,860
Ma...

157
00:05:59,901 --> 00:06:01,069
Dad, what?

158
00:06:01,111 --> 00:06:02,403
We have to pretend
that we're married?!

159
00:06:02,445 --> 00:06:04,865
Son, I had to shave
my ears for tonight.

160
00:06:04,906 --> 00:06:05,991
You can do this.

161
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:09,077
Can you believe that?

162
00:06:09,119 --> 00:06:10,120
Yeah, I know.

163
00:06:10,162 --> 00:06:11,496
If you're going
to do the ears

164
00:06:11,538 --> 00:06:14,415
you may as well take
a pass at the nosal area.

165
00:06:14,457 --> 00:06:16,084
No, no.

166
00:06:16,126 --> 00:06:17,919
I don't want to have to lie
about us being married.

167
00:06:17,961 --> 00:06:19,504
Oh, no, I know.
I don't either.

168
00:06:19,546 --> 00:06:22,090
But you know what, it's their
party, and it's one night

169
00:06:22,132 --> 00:06:23,550
and we don't
even have to lie.

170
00:06:23,591 --> 00:06:24,843
We just
won't say anything.

171
00:06:24,885 --> 00:06:26,761
If it comes up again,
we'll just...

172
00:06:26,803 --> 00:06:28,429
we'll smile,
we'll nod along.

173
00:06:28,471 --> 00:06:29,639
Ross.
Rachel.

174
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:31,683
Hi, Aunt Lisa.

175
00:06:31,725 --> 00:06:33,185
Uncle Dan.

176
00:06:33,227 --> 00:06:35,436
Congratulations
on the baby.

177
00:06:35,478 --> 00:06:36,646
And on
the wedding.

178
00:06:36,688 --> 00:06:38,148
BOTH:
Mm.

179
00:06:39,524 --> 00:06:41,235
Here's a little something
to get you started.

180
00:06:41,276 --> 00:06:42,527
Oh.

181
00:06:42,569 --> 00:06:44,154
So how's married
life treating you?

182
00:06:44,196 --> 00:06:45,364
Unbelievable.

183
00:06:45,404 --> 00:06:46,614
Love marriage.

184
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:47,949
Great.

185
00:06:47,991 --> 00:06:49,617
We'll see you later.

186
00:06:49,659 --> 00:06:50,785
Hey.

187
00:06:50,827 --> 00:06:51,995
Hey.
Hey.

188
00:06:52,037 --> 00:06:54,122
What a beautiful place.

189
00:06:54,164 --> 00:06:55,665
What a great night.

190
00:06:55,707 --> 00:06:57,125
I have to tell you

191
00:06:57,167 --> 00:07:00,337
being here with all of you in
Event Room C...

192
00:07:02,005 --> 00:07:04,132
I feel so lucky.

193
00:07:04,174 --> 00:07:06,801
To think of all the good times
that have happened here:

194
00:07:06,843 --> 00:07:09,637
the birthdays, the proms,
the mitzvahs--

195
00:07:09,679 --> 00:07:11,014
both bar and bat.

196
00:07:13,225 --> 00:07:15,143
But none of it
will compare with tonight.

197
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:17,229
My God, I don't want
to forget this moment.

198
00:07:17,271 --> 00:07:20,481
It's like I want to take
a mental picture of you all.

199
00:07:20,523 --> 00:07:21,608
Click!

200
00:07:23,193 --> 00:07:25,028
I don't think
the flash went off.

201
00:07:25,070 --> 00:07:27,197
( laughs )

202
00:07:29,866 --> 00:07:31,159
I'm going to go find
the men's room.

203
00:07:31,201 --> 00:07:32,535
I'll be right back.
I'll go with you.

204
00:07:32,577 --> 00:07:34,037
Somewhere there is someone

205
00:07:34,079 --> 00:07:36,748
with a tranquilizer gun
and a huge butterfly net

206
00:07:36,790 --> 00:07:38,083
looking for that man.

207
00:07:39,459 --> 00:07:41,544
I have to go to the bathroom,
too

208
00:07:41,586 --> 00:07:44,505
but I don't want him
complimenting my thing.

209
00:07:44,547 --> 00:07:46,383
I'm so glad we
weren't in the car.

210
00:07:46,425 --> 00:07:47,717
Did he ever let up?

211
00:07:47,759 --> 00:07:49,552
He called
the Long Island Expressway

212
00:07:49,594 --> 00:07:50,637
a concrete miracle.

213
00:07:50,678 --> 00:07:52,389
( groaning )

214
00:07:52,431 --> 00:07:54,975
( mockingly ):
This room, this night!

215
00:07:55,016 --> 00:07:56,684
That waiter!

216
00:07:56,726 --> 00:07:58,061
His shoes!

217
00:07:58,103 --> 00:08:01,189
I must take a mental picture.

218
00:08:01,231 --> 00:08:02,690
Oops, sorry.

219
00:08:07,070 --> 00:08:09,448
Were you guys making
fun of Parker?

220
00:08:10,531 --> 00:08:11,574
That depends.

221
00:08:11,616 --> 00:08:13,618
How much did you hear?

222
00:08:15,287 --> 00:08:16,955
Well, he's a little
enthusiastic.

223
00:08:16,997 --> 00:08:18,248
What's wrong with that?

224
00:08:18,290 --> 00:08:20,459
It's just, it's,
it's so much.

225
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,294
Well, so what?
I like him.

226
00:08:22,336 --> 00:08:24,503
Do I make fun
of the people
that you've dated?

227
00:08:24,545 --> 00:08:25,505
Tag?

228
00:08:25,546 --> 00:08:27,048
Janice? Mona?

229
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:29,508
No, because friends
don't do that.

230
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:31,510
But do you want my opinion?
You want it?

231
00:08:31,552 --> 00:08:34,473
'Cause in my opinion,
your collective dating record

232
00:08:34,514 --> 00:08:36,808
reads like a "Who's Who"
of human crap.

233
00:08:39,394 --> 00:08:40,645
Oh, I feel terrible.

234
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:42,814
I know.

235
00:08:43,857 --> 00:08:45,817
What was wrong with Mona?

236
00:08:50,571 --> 00:08:51,948
Open it, open it,
open it.

237
00:08:51,990 --> 00:08:53,283
Yeah, baby.

238
00:08:53,325 --> 00:08:57,287
So, we never got to hear
about your wedding.

239
00:08:57,329 --> 00:08:59,664
We were surprised
we weren't invited.

240
00:08:59,706 --> 00:09:01,041
Oh, no, no, no.

241
00:09:01,082 --> 00:09:02,667
It was just our parents,
and one or two fri...

242
00:09:02,709 --> 00:09:03,751
It was a small wedding.

243
00:09:03,793 --> 00:09:05,253
But it was beautiful.

244
00:09:05,295 --> 00:09:09,341
I mean, it was small,
but kind of spectacular.

245
00:09:09,383 --> 00:09:10,175
Where did you have it?

246
00:09:10,217 --> 00:09:12,302
On a cliff in Barbados.

247
00:09:12,344 --> 00:09:13,887
At sunset.

248
00:09:13,928 --> 00:09:16,806
And Stevie Wonder
sang "Isn't She Lovely"

249
00:09:16,848 --> 00:09:18,641
as I walked
down the aisle.

250
00:09:18,683 --> 00:09:19,809
Really?

251
00:09:19,851 --> 00:09:21,520
Yeah, Stevie's
an old family friend.

252
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:22,521
Oh, my God.

253
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:23,730
That sounds amazing.

254
00:09:23,771 --> 00:09:25,273
I'd love to see pictures.

255
00:09:25,315 --> 00:09:26,983
Yeah, so would I.

256
00:09:28,026 --> 00:09:30,153
You wouldn't think
that Annie Leibowitz

257
00:09:30,195 --> 00:09:32,364
would forget to put
film in the camera.

258
00:09:32,406 --> 00:09:34,824
( chuckles )

259
00:09:34,866 --> 00:09:37,660
Would you, would you
excuse for a second?

260
00:09:40,330 --> 00:09:42,332
Um, what are you doing?

261
00:09:42,374 --> 00:09:43,583
What? I'm not you.

262
00:09:43,624 --> 00:09:45,335
This may be the only
wedding I ever have.

263
00:09:47,921 --> 00:09:49,506
And I want it
to be amazing.

264
00:09:49,548 --> 00:09:51,174
Okay, okay... ooh!

265
00:09:51,216 --> 00:09:53,593
Ooh, maybe I rode in
on a Harley.

266
00:09:53,634 --> 00:09:55,970
Okay, Ross,
it has to be realistic.

267
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:02,894
Say, uh, are you okay?

268
00:10:02,936 --> 00:10:05,105
You seem... kind of quiet.

269
00:10:05,146 --> 00:10:06,106
No, I'm fine.

270
00:10:06,147 --> 00:10:07,774
I'm great.
I'm with you.

271
00:10:07,815 --> 00:10:08,900
And I'm with you.

272
00:10:08,942 --> 00:10:11,027
What a great time to be alive.

273
00:10:12,362 --> 00:10:14,155
Look at this
plate bouncy thing.

274
00:10:16,366 --> 00:10:19,119
What an inspired solution to
man's plate dispensing problems.

275
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:20,120
Mm-hmm, yeah.

276
00:10:20,161 --> 00:10:21,121
Ah!

277
00:10:21,162 --> 00:10:22,539
Oysters.

278
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:23,622
Let me feed you one.

279
00:10:23,664 --> 00:10:24,749
Oh, that's not necessary.

280
00:10:24,791 --> 00:10:25,833
Please.

281
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:26,876
No, actually,
I don't...

282
00:10:26,918 --> 00:10:27,668
I won't quit
until you try one.

283
00:10:27,710 --> 00:10:28,878
Okay, fine. Fine.

284
00:10:31,131 --> 00:10:32,132
Mmm. Mm-hmm.

285
00:10:32,173 --> 00:10:33,383
That was good.

286
00:10:33,425 --> 00:10:35,760
What are they like?
I've never had one.

287
00:10:37,554 --> 00:10:38,846
Why don't you just try one?

288
00:10:38,888 --> 00:10:40,140
Nah, they look too weird.

289
00:10:43,017 --> 00:10:44,311
What are you doing?

290
00:10:44,352 --> 00:10:46,020
Just going over
my toast.

291
00:10:46,062 --> 00:10:48,064
Those two will never
know what hit them.

292
00:10:48,106 --> 00:10:49,190
I can't wait.

293
00:10:49,232 --> 00:10:51,109
They're going to be
crying so hard

294
00:10:51,151 --> 00:10:53,694
they'll be fighting
for breath.

295
00:10:53,736 --> 00:10:54,779
You know,
if you want to

296
00:10:54,821 --> 00:10:55,780
I can just
hold them down

297
00:10:55,822 --> 00:10:56,781
and you can just...

298
00:10:56,823 --> 00:10:57,824
( grunting )

299
00:10:59,534 --> 00:11:01,536
And my-my veil was lace

300
00:11:01,578 --> 00:11:04,705
made by blind Belgian nuns.

301
00:11:04,747 --> 00:11:05,706
Blind?

302
00:11:05,748 --> 00:11:07,208
Well, not at first-- but it

303
00:11:07,250 --> 00:11:08,585
was very intricate work

304
00:11:08,627 --> 00:11:10,211
and they said even though

305
00:11:10,253 --> 00:11:11,546
they lost their sight

306
00:11:11,588 --> 00:11:12,880
it was all worth it.

307
00:11:12,922 --> 00:11:15,300
I bet you looked
beautiful.

308
00:11:15,342 --> 00:11:16,759
Oh, well, I-I don't know
about that

309
00:11:16,801 --> 00:11:18,345
but there were
some people that said

310
00:11:18,386 --> 00:11:20,639
I looked like a floating angel.

311
00:11:22,056 --> 00:11:23,682
How did you
propose?

312
00:11:23,724 --> 00:11:27,686
Oh! Yeah, that's
a great story.

313
00:11:27,728 --> 00:11:32,150
Well, um, actually I-I took her
to the planetarium.

314
00:11:32,192 --> 00:11:34,735
That's-that's where
we had our first date.

315
00:11:34,777 --> 00:11:40,116
Um, she walked in and I had
the room filled with lilies

316
00:11:40,158 --> 00:11:41,159
her favorite flower.

317
00:11:41,201 --> 00:11:42,743
Oh, that is
so sweet.

318
00:11:42,785 --> 00:11:44,538
Ssh! I want to hear the rest!

319
00:11:47,123 --> 00:11:50,627
Then Fred Astaire singing
"The Way You Look Tonight"

320
00:11:50,669 --> 00:11:51,961
came on the sound system

321
00:11:52,003 --> 00:11:53,754
and the lights came down

322
00:11:53,796 --> 00:11:55,382
and I-I got on one knee

323
00:11:55,423 --> 00:11:56,383
and written

324
00:11:56,424 --> 00:11:58,677
across the dome in the stars

325
00:11:58,717 --> 00:11:59,760
were the words

326
00:11:59,802 --> 00:12:01,221
"Will you marry me?"

327
00:12:01,262 --> 00:12:03,056
( group sighing )

328
00:12:03,097 --> 00:12:07,561
And the ring was the size
of my fist.

329
00:12:08,811 --> 00:12:10,104
Hey, uh, Phoebe...

330
00:12:10,146 --> 00:12:14,067
look, um...

331
00:12:14,108 --> 00:12:16,528
I want to apologize about
before, okay?

332
00:12:16,570 --> 00:12:17,820
We were being jerks.

333
00:12:17,862 --> 00:12:19,030
Parker's a nice guy

334
00:12:19,072 --> 00:12:20,281
and I'd like to get
to know him.

335
00:12:20,323 --> 00:12:22,409
Then you better
do it now.
Why?

336
00:12:22,450 --> 00:12:25,036
I'm going to
kill him.
What?

337
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:26,913
You guys were
right; he's just

338
00:12:26,954 --> 00:12:29,123
too excited about
everything.

339
00:12:29,165 --> 00:12:30,958
I mean, I'm all
for living life

340
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,878
but this is the Gellers'
35th anniversary, okay?

341
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:36,590
Let's call a spade a spade:
this party stinks.

342
00:12:36,631 --> 00:12:38,883
I know-- I'm having
the worst time.

343
00:12:38,925 --> 00:12:41,636
There was a 15-minute line
for the buffet

344
00:12:41,678 --> 00:12:43,638
and when I finally
got up to the plates

345
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:47,434
I slipped
on a giant booger.

346
00:12:47,475 --> 00:12:52,272
Are you sure it
wasn't an oyster?

347
00:12:52,313 --> 00:12:54,315
I guess it
could've been.

348
00:12:54,357 --> 00:12:56,067
I didn't really
look at it, you know?

349
00:12:56,109 --> 00:12:57,736
I just wiped it
on Chandler's coat

350
00:12:57,776 --> 00:12:59,738
and got the hell
out of there.

351
00:12:59,778 --> 00:13:02,449
I just thought he was
such a great guy.

352
00:13:02,490 --> 00:13:04,367
I was so excited
about him.

353
00:13:04,409 --> 00:13:07,995
Well, hey, you know, you should
be excited about him.

354
00:13:08,037 --> 00:13:10,624
There's nothing wrong with him.
He's a good guy.

355
00:13:10,665 --> 00:13:11,625
You think?

356
00:13:11,666 --> 00:13:12,959
Yeah, you know
what I think?

357
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,336
I think we were all
just being too negative.

358
00:13:15,378 --> 00:13:17,338
You're right...
you're right.

359
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:19,173
He's just embracing life.

360
00:13:19,215 --> 00:13:21,676
We could all stand to be
a little more like Parker.

361
00:13:21,718 --> 00:13:23,762
You know what,
I am like him.

362
00:13:23,802 --> 00:13:25,846
I'm a sunny,
positive person.

363
00:13:25,888 --> 00:13:28,182
Uh, actually, you have
a little bit of an edge.

364
00:13:28,224 --> 00:13:29,350
What's that, now?

365
00:13:31,269 --> 00:13:32,520
Oh, it's Parker.

366
00:13:32,562 --> 00:13:33,854
Look... the bunny hop.

367
00:13:33,896 --> 00:13:35,106
( gasps ):
Oh, I love it!

368
00:13:35,148 --> 00:13:36,107
You do?

369
00:13:36,149 --> 00:13:37,609
Are you kidding?

370
00:13:37,651 --> 00:13:40,069
People acting like animals
to music? Come on!

371
00:13:41,070 --> 00:13:42,196
( ringing glass )

372
00:13:42,238 --> 00:13:44,073
Okay, it's time for the toast.

373
00:13:44,115 --> 00:13:47,535
I know that normally
Ross gives the toast

374
00:13:47,577 --> 00:13:49,454
but this year
I'm going to do it.

375
00:13:49,496 --> 00:13:50,622
( audience groaning )

376
00:13:50,664 --> 00:13:52,749
No, no, it-it

377
00:13:52,791 --> 00:13:55,543
it's going to be great, really.

378
00:13:55,585 --> 00:13:56,877
Um, okay.

379
00:13:56,919 --> 00:14:02,216
Um, Mom, Dad, uh,
when I got married

380
00:14:02,258 --> 00:14:04,927
one of the things that made me
sure that I could do it

381
00:14:04,969 --> 00:14:08,765
was the amazing example
the two of you set for me.

382
00:14:08,807 --> 00:14:11,100
For that, and for
so many other things

383
00:14:11,142 --> 00:14:12,644
I want to say thank you.

384
00:14:12,686 --> 00:14:17,148
I know I probably don't say it
enough, but...

385
00:14:17,190 --> 00:14:19,442
I love you.

386
00:14:28,827 --> 00:14:32,497
Uh... w-when I look around
this room

387
00:14:32,539 --> 00:14:34,666
I'm-I'm saddened by the thought

388
00:14:34,708 --> 00:14:36,959
of those who could not
be with us.

389
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:41,715
Nana, my beloved grandmother,
who would so want to be here

390
00:14:41,756 --> 00:14:44,884
but she can't...

391
00:14:44,925 --> 00:14:46,886
because she's dead.

392
00:14:50,515 --> 00:14:52,016
As...

393
00:14:52,058 --> 00:14:53,560
is our dog, Chi-Chi.

394
00:14:55,478 --> 00:14:58,606
Look how cute she is... was.

395
00:15:01,067 --> 00:15:03,778
Do me a favor and pass this
to my parents?

396
00:15:03,820 --> 00:15:09,116
Remember, um, she's dead, okay?
Her and Nana, gone.

397
00:15:11,870 --> 00:15:13,996
Wow.

398
00:15:14,038 --> 00:15:16,416
( sighing )

399
00:15:16,457 --> 00:15:18,501
Um...

400
00:15:18,543 --> 00:15:20,002
Hey--

401
00:15:20,044 --> 00:15:22,380
does anybody remember
when Debra Winger

402
00:15:22,422 --> 00:15:24,298
had to say good-bye
to her children

403
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:25,717
in Terms of Endearment?

404
00:15:27,886 --> 00:15:30,304
Didn't see that?

405
00:15:30,346 --> 00:15:33,850
No movie fans?

406
00:15:33,892 --> 00:15:36,686
Uh, y-you want to hear
something sad?

407
00:15:36,728 --> 00:15:38,979
Um, the other day I was watching
60 Minutes

408
00:15:39,021 --> 00:15:41,900
and there was this piece
on these orphans in Romania

409
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:43,777
who had been so neglected

410
00:15:43,818 --> 00:15:46,237
that they were
incapable of love.

411
00:15:52,827 --> 00:15:55,747
You people are made of stone!

412
00:15:55,789 --> 00:15:58,332
Here's to Mom and Dad. Whatever.

413
00:16:05,673 --> 00:16:09,928
Thank you, Monica.
That was, uh,
interesting.

414
00:16:09,968 --> 00:16:11,053
Wasn't that
interesting,
Jack?

415
00:16:11,095 --> 00:16:13,848
Why don't I
remember this dog?

416
00:16:17,143 --> 00:16:20,146
Ross, why don't you
give us your toast now?

417
00:16:20,187 --> 00:16:23,441
Oh, no, Mom, it's just Monica
this year.

418
00:16:23,483 --> 00:16:24,776
You're not

419
00:16:24,818 --> 00:16:26,402
going to say anything

420
00:16:26,444 --> 00:16:28,905
on our 35th wedding anniversary?

421
00:16:28,947 --> 00:16:32,158
No, of-of course. um...

422
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:35,787
Um, everybody?

423
00:16:35,829 --> 00:16:37,121
( clears throat):
Um...

424
00:16:37,163 --> 00:16:39,666
I-I just wanted to say

425
00:16:39,707 --> 00:16:43,962
uh, on behalf of my new bride,
Rachel...

426
00:16:47,089 --> 00:16:48,633
and myself, um...

427
00:16:48,675 --> 00:16:51,636
that if...

428
00:16:51,678 --> 00:16:56,975
if in 35 years we're half
as happy as you guys are

429
00:16:57,016 --> 00:17:00,645
we'll count ourselves the
luckiest people in the world.

430
00:17:00,687 --> 00:17:04,190
( crying ):
Oh, Ross.

431
00:17:06,275 --> 00:17:11,865
I just wish Nana were alive
to hear Ross's toast.

432
00:17:17,620 --> 00:17:18,955
My God

433
00:17:18,997 --> 00:17:23,167
what a fantastically
well-lit hallway.

434
00:17:23,209 --> 00:17:25,294
Can I, um, get you something
to drink?

435
00:17:25,336 --> 00:17:27,588
Like a water and Valium?

436
00:17:27,630 --> 00:17:29,256
I must say,
you know

437
00:17:29,298 --> 00:17:31,676
this apartment,
it's-it's...

438
00:17:31,718 --> 00:17:33,011
There are no words.

439
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:33,761
Oh, thank God.

440
00:17:33,803 --> 00:17:34,554
It's a haven.

441
00:17:35,930 --> 00:17:37,724
A third-floor paradise,
a modern-day Eden

442
00:17:37,765 --> 00:17:38,766
in the midst of...

443
00:17:38,808 --> 00:17:40,226
Yeah, I know, I know, uh-huh.

444
00:17:40,267 --> 00:17:41,352
I got you, oh, yeah.

445
00:17:41,394 --> 00:17:42,937
Listen, let's, um...

446
00:17:42,979 --> 00:17:45,356
Why don't
we just sit and relax?

447
00:17:45,398 --> 00:17:47,441
You know, just be
with each other quietly?

448
00:17:47,483 --> 00:17:48,442
Yeah.

449
00:17:48,484 --> 00:17:50,987
That sounds great.

450
00:17:51,029 --> 00:17:53,322
My God, this is the most
comfortable couch

451
00:17:53,364 --> 00:17:54,991
I've ever sat on
in my entire life.

452
00:17:55,033 --> 00:17:56,826
All right, let's
try something else.

453
00:17:56,868 --> 00:17:58,411
Let's play a game.

454
00:17:58,452 --> 00:17:59,328
I love games.

455
00:17:59,370 --> 00:18:01,330
Shocking.

456
00:18:01,372 --> 00:18:03,833
Um, let's play the game of...

457
00:18:03,875 --> 00:18:08,337
( chuckling ):
who can stay quiet the longest?

458
00:18:08,379 --> 00:18:11,799
Ah... or Jenga.

459
00:18:11,841 --> 00:18:14,844
Let's play this one first.

460
00:18:14,886 --> 00:18:18,097
And remember, whoever
talks first loses.

461
00:18:22,769 --> 00:18:24,020
I lose, now Jenga.

462
00:18:24,062 --> 00:18:27,314
Oh, my God, my God.

463
00:18:27,356 --> 00:18:29,650
Is something wrong?

464
00:18:29,692 --> 00:18:31,611
Wrong? Really, you
know the word wrong?

465
00:18:31,652 --> 00:18:34,238
Everything isn't perfect,
everything isn't magical?

466
00:18:34,280 --> 00:18:35,698
Everything isn't aglow

467
00:18:35,740 --> 00:18:38,034
with the light of
a million fairies?

468
00:18:38,076 --> 00:18:40,745
They were just brake
lights, Parker!

469
00:18:40,787 --> 00:18:44,582
Well, excuse me for putting
a good spin on a traffic jam.

470
00:18:44,624 --> 00:18:46,876
Okay, but-but,
you don't have

471
00:18:46,918 --> 00:18:49,003
to put a good spin
on everything.

472
00:18:49,045 --> 00:18:50,129
I'm sorry,
that's who I am--

473
00:18:50,170 --> 00:18:51,255
I'm a positive person.

474
00:18:51,297 --> 00:18:52,256
No.

475
00:18:52,298 --> 00:18:53,883
I'm a positive person.

476
00:18:53,925 --> 00:18:56,844
You are like Santa
Claus on Prozac...

477
00:18:56,886 --> 00:19:02,224
at Disneyland...
getting laid.

478
00:19:02,266 --> 00:19:03,893
So, what do
you want me to do?

479
00:19:03,935 --> 00:19:05,645
You want me to be
more negative?

480
00:19:05,686 --> 00:19:07,271
You want me
to be less happy?

481
00:19:07,313 --> 00:19:09,565
Be much less happy.

482
00:19:09,607 --> 00:19:10,984
Fine.

483
00:19:11,025 --> 00:19:13,861
Well, then to quote Ross,
"I'd better be going."

484
00:19:15,321 --> 00:19:16,948
So long.

485
00:19:16,990 --> 00:19:17,949
Hey, don't let

486
00:19:17,991 --> 00:19:19,492
the best door in the world

487
00:19:19,533 --> 00:19:21,619
hit you in the ass
on your way out.

488
00:19:23,955 --> 00:19:25,623
( knocking )

489
00:19:26,916 --> 00:19:29,002
Isn't this the most incredible
fight you've ever had

490
00:19:29,043 --> 00:19:30,003
in your entire life?

491
00:19:30,044 --> 00:19:31,879
( slams door )

492
00:19:34,423 --> 00:19:37,718
And then we could have gone
from the ceremony

493
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:40,763
to the reception
with you in the sidecar.

494
00:19:40,805 --> 00:19:42,849
Ross, it just wouldn't
have been feasible.

495
00:19:42,890 --> 00:19:46,268
But having a dove place the ring
on your finger

496
00:19:46,310 --> 00:19:47,603
would have been no problem.

497
00:19:49,939 --> 00:19:52,025
It was really fun being married
to you tonight.

498
00:19:52,066 --> 00:19:55,319
Yeah, and it was the easiest
400 bucks I ever made.

499
00:19:55,361 --> 00:19:56,737
( laughs )

500
00:19:56,779 --> 00:19:59,699
Okay, Ross, can I, um,
can I ask you something?

501
00:19:59,740 --> 00:20:00,992
Yeah.

502
00:20:01,034 --> 00:20:02,284
That proposal
at the planetarium?

503
00:20:02,326 --> 00:20:03,285
I know

504
00:20:03,327 --> 00:20:05,287
I know,
it was stupid.

505
00:20:05,329 --> 00:20:06,622
Are you kidding?

506
00:20:06,664 --> 00:20:08,457
With the...
with the lilies

507
00:20:08,499 --> 00:20:10,710
and the song
and the stars?

508
00:20:10,751 --> 00:20:12,003
It was...

509
00:20:12,045 --> 00:20:15,506
It was really wonderful.

510
00:20:15,548 --> 00:20:16,674
You just make that up?

511
00:20:16,716 --> 00:20:18,676
No. Actually,
I thought about it

512
00:20:18,718 --> 00:20:20,178
when we were going out.

513
00:20:20,219 --> 00:20:23,806
That's how I imagined
I would ask you to marry me.

514
00:20:23,848 --> 00:20:26,684
Wow.

515
00:20:26,726 --> 00:20:29,812
Well, that would have been...

516
00:20:29,854 --> 00:20:33,149
that would have been
very hard to say no to.

517
00:20:35,735 --> 00:20:37,403
Well, it's a good
thing I didn't do it

518
00:20:37,444 --> 00:20:39,321
because it sounds
like it would have been

519
00:20:39,363 --> 00:20:41,490
a very expensive wedding.

520
00:20:41,532 --> 00:20:44,118
( laughs )

521
00:20:44,160 --> 00:20:46,079
Mmm.

522
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:48,455
Okay.

523
00:20:48,497 --> 00:20:54,420
Good night.
Good night.

524
00:20:54,461 --> 00:20:56,630
Uh, even if the sidecar
had a windscreen

525
00:20:56,672 --> 00:20:58,632
so your hair wouldn't
get messed up?

526
00:20:58,674 --> 00:20:59,633
I will think about it.

527
00:20:59,675 --> 00:21:05,890
That's all
I'm asking.

528
00:21:05,932 --> 00:21:07,225
Okay, that's it,
I give up--

529
00:21:07,265 --> 00:21:09,102
at Mom and Dad's
40th anniversary

530
00:21:09,143 --> 00:21:11,062
you're the one
giving the speech.

531
00:21:11,104 --> 00:21:13,606
You know, I don't
understand why
they didn't cry.

532
00:21:13,647 --> 00:21:15,983
It was a beautiful speech.

533
00:21:16,025 --> 00:21:17,359
Oh, come on.
Hey--

534
00:21:17,401 --> 00:21:19,195
all that stuff you
said about true love?

535
00:21:19,237 --> 00:21:20,404
You were right.

536
00:21:20,446 --> 00:21:22,322
I mean, we did
learn a lot

537
00:21:22,364 --> 00:21:23,616
from Mom and Dad.

538
00:21:23,657 --> 00:21:25,826
And that picture
of Chi-Chi

539
00:21:25,868 --> 00:21:28,204
with her mischievous
grin...

540
00:21:28,246 --> 00:21:29,622
And what you said
about Nana?

541
00:21:29,663 --> 00:21:32,875
Oh, yeah, she
really would
have wanted

542
00:21:32,917 --> 00:21:34,293
to be there.

543
00:21:34,334 --> 00:21:38,589
And you know what?

544
00:21:38,631 --> 00:21:40,800
I think she was.

545
00:21:41,884 --> 00:21:44,971
Oh, good God, Ross--
how the hell do you do it?

546
00:21:45,012 --> 00:21:48,599
